{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1552444-how-many-chorus-choristers-know-the-\u201ctruth\u201d-of-mithras","id":1552444,"title":"How many Chorus Choristers know the \u201ctruth\u201d(?) of Mithras?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-07-16T02:51:20-07:00","id":1552444,"title":"How many Chorus Choristers know the \u201ctruth\u201d(?) of Mithras?","content":"Emmm I mean how many Choristers know that Ventrue Methuselah guy falsely uses their worshipping gods\u2019 name, or most don\u2019t know this? Or most Choristers simply don\u2019t care this? But I guess some of them may serve him (knowingly or not)<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Although in Dark Age Mage Grimoire (page 38) it seems that his effort did influenced them:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://imgse.com/i/pV1q2Bn\" target=\"_blank\"><img data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://s21.ax1x.com/2025/07/16/pV1q2Bn.jpg\" alt=\"\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://s21.ax1x.com/2025/07/16/pV1q2Bn.jpg\" data-thumb-url=\"https://s21.ax1x.com/2025/07/16/pV1q2Bn.jpg\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/></a><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> It seems that this acolyte Soroush may have been ghouled though<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24560-rock113","name":"Rock113","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24560_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-16T03:20:56-07:00","id":1552445,"title":"","content":"This would have little meaningful impact on the modern Chorus. The big reason in that in order for the Tradition for form and unify a plethora of mages with distinct faith-systems into one group, they had do adopt a stance that basically amounts to no Sleeper Faith being &quot;true&quot; and just imperfect reflections of the &quot;truth&quot; of The One. While this might allow Mithraism to survive in the Chorus far longer than it did in the real world (frankly the idea of their being enough of them to mention in the Dark Ages is pretty far-fetched as it is, as Christians actively and rather successfully fought to wipe out the Cults of Mithras as Christianity ascended to power and the Messianic Voices are otherwise presented as far more dedicated to the Abrahamic Faiths and Zoroastrianism than makes sense of them to let cults worshiping a &quot;pagan idol&quot; in), it would also mean that being overly dedicated to a specific faith instead of religion being the foundation to build a better understand of the one would be very antithetical to Chorus members after the Tradition settled into its new normal.<br /> <br /> Since Mithrasism was a small group within the Voices, it's not some massive shock to the Chorus that some mages got duped by a powerful vampire if anyone them actually knew that happened (which there's no evidence to suggest).<br /> <br /> That specific side-bar is also massively out of step with both VtM presentations of Mithras-the-vampire (who you'd think would have done something with a cult of mages worshiping him even if that never comes up on the VtM side of things), and Mage outside of the Dark Ages material. However of an intrigue little plot hook to explore if a group was inspired, absolutely nothing in the rest of the books seems to acknowledge this concept as being an issue (which isn't that surprising, DA Mage was fairly late in the original WoD run, and M20 seems inclined to ignore it for the most part).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-16T15:33:01-07:00","id":1552462,"title":"","content":"Probably very few Choristers know about Mithras-the-Vampire, if any, and it is unlikely that they're Mithraics themselves.<br /> <br /> Even for those who know that means little. Mithras isn't the only Vampire (much less supernatural person in general) claiming to be a god, and that hardly matters. His power and his devoted cults may be a problem, but that's regardless of which god he pretends to be. It also has very little to do with the possibility of Mithras-the-God actually existing in some of the several possible forms for gods to exist in Mage, so the devoted (Choristers or not) wouldn't be really impacted by the information.<br /> <br /> You can run this plot with a group of Mages that follow Mithraism. And they don't have to be Choristers (as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> said, this side-bar is quite out of step with them), in fact they're more probably Orphans or a Craft. But outside a specific plot, it's unlikely that any Mage would particularly care about Vampire Mithras calling himself a god. They would probably care about a Methuselah of such age existing and doing stuff regardless, but if not, it isn't his name what will change anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-16T15:35:39-07:00","id":1552463,"title":"","content":"Of course, the Mages might assume that the Vampire was a liar or deluded.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-16T16:08:04-07:00","id":1552465,"title":"","content":"Unless they want to find out due to sheer curiosity, this is the likely outcome. There's zero reason to believe this Vampire is the actual origin of the cult of Mithras.<br /> <br /> That doesn't means it isn't likely this vampire is the source of <i>some</i> cults, or that he controls some of them regardless. But then, this comes with the territory when you're dealing with Methuselah, it isn't a big change.<br /> <br /> Also, given how god-like entities work in Mage, <b>even</b> if Mithras-the-Vampire is the original Mithras, Mithras-the-God may still exist as a completely independent entity, and in fact may have retroactively existed before this first cause. The very nature of the Mage setting makes things complex beyond the person of this vampire.<br /> <br /> Now, Mages being what they are, some of them may decide to investigate this further, look into the origins of the cult of Mithras and each of its cells through history. But that's again regardless of their Traditions, beliefs and allegiances. And regardless if they'll find he's lying or not, or what they theorize about it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-18T03:50:17-07:00","id":1552510,"title":"","content":"I always liked Mithras in this regard<br /> Mithras, the Roman God has roots in the ancient Indo-Iranian god Mitra, who appears in written sources as early as the 14th century BC in an alliance treaty between the Hittites and Mitanni. In the Vedic (Indian) and Zoroastrian (Iranian) texts, Mithra is depicted as a solar deity and a god of justice and contracts. <br /> This was nicely aligned the fact, the Vampire Mithras - was born in the territory where Iran is now located, and served in the military as a general of one of Indo-Iranic tribes, was youth in India - was Embraced in1258 BC and Mithras believed himself a god from the start (but it would take some time before he convinced others.)<br /> He maybe considered hismelf as an avatar of Mithra, who knows, but started to call himself Mithras (or was even his original name) and slowly a cult formed around him, and from the sheer Faith the God Mithras was born in the High Umbra <br /> At least this is my take on the matter, in a sense both parties were and are right, Mithras was not a God... but his cult gave birth to the God<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-18T05:09:44-07:00","id":1552511,"title":"","content":"There are many differences between the Iranian Mithra and the Roman Mithras, so many that the scholar consensus is that it is unlikely that there was any real continuity between them except in the (misguided) perception of the Romans. Those versions of Mithas didn't only have very different dispositions, they had distinct portfolios, iconography, rituals, everything. And Mithras-the-Vampire has much more to do with the Roman god than the Iranian. The Vedic Mitra is far more similar to (and probably has continuity with) the Iranian than the Roman.<br /> <br /> So I personally think Mithras probably took his name from the god as worshiped in his living culture, where it was worshiped since at least a century before he was even born, but he never really thought of this cult as being about him, they &quot;got everything wrong&quot;. He only really started his own cult (successfully, at least) in Rome, and effectively had nothing to do with the Iranian god except by being Iranian himself and the Romans knowing where he came from.<br /> <br /> Mages that follow the Roman Mithras would be, indeed, inheritors of this vampire-led cult (regardless if their particular cell is linked to the vampire or not). But those who revere Mithra through Zoroastrianism have nothing to do with him.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-18T12:48:46-07:00","id":1552520,"title":"","content":"And it's important to note that the Messianic Voices that were supposedly drawn to Mithras-the-vampire were <i>Persian</i> not European. Arsalan, the full mage, rejected the &quot;Mithras&quot; he found in the British isles. It was his acolyte (either unawakened, or low level apprentice in DA terms since terms were looser then), that got sucked into believing they had found the true Mithra and eventually (aka in the now of the DA timeline) came back to the Middle East to preach this to other Zoroastrian and Mithra-follower Voices.<br /> <br /> So, while it's not hard to accommodate that some Messianic Voices still followed the Roman Mithras cult practices into the &quot;now&quot; of the setting despite it being centuries after the cults ceased to show up in history with the rise of Christianity and the fading of the western Roman Empire; it is rather hard to square the idea that all of them got skipped over (despite being more inclined to the Mithras we see in VtM by 1066) for a Persian mage that saw through Mithras-the-vampire.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-18T21:15:14-07:00","id":1552524,"title":"","content":"By the way, one thing that should be clear, as it pertain to the real world:<br /> <br /> While the Roman Mithraism was completely extinguished IRL with the rise of Christianity, the Iranian Mithra is still worshiped today by some Zoroastrians. And it isn't a revival, either. While we can't say for sure how much its practices and beliefs changed through millennia, it's still a full continuity of the ancient cult.<br /> <br /> The Vedic Mitra too still exists, although his influence decline a lot within Hinduism, but he's still included in many rituals and festivals related to the sun or the morning.<br /> <br /> So, when we talk about the Roman Mithras represented within the setting by Mithras-the-Vampire, we're talking about a mystery cult that existed in Rome no earlier than the 1st century BCE and endured only up to the early 5th century at most, then got erased from popular knowledge for about 15 centuries. When we talk about the Indo-Iranian god, we're talking about a deity with mostly continuing worship from at least the 15th century BCE to today, always as part of mainstream religions in the area. They're very different beasts to use in the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-18T23:34:01-07:00","id":1552525,"title":"","content":"I think there's an issue with what's stated in the books vs. reality and which should win in this. The books (esp. DA Mage Grimoire, where the sidebar in question came from) treat Persian Mithraic veneration as on par in social importance with Roman Mithras cults; rather than just a part of some Zoroastrian beliefs (the book actually treats Mithraic Voices and Zoroastrian Voices as distinctly different groups).<br /> <br /> While not terribly difficult to ignore the books for something a bit more reflective of reality, the WoD takes a lot of liberties if people want to do something ahistoric.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-19T08:45:17-07:00","id":1552530,"title":"","content":"I just think those are relevant things to keep in mind, as in there's a difference between making something aware that it's a departure and making it in total ignorance. It's also relevant insofar as you may decide differently based on something being a &quot;dead&quot; or living religion IRL.<br /> <br /> Some STs, and even players (especially in Mage), may want to work with the actual modern Zoroastrianism, and how the cult of Mithra would look like in its WoD iteration. A Craft based on either the Parsi or the Irani communities will be quite different if the role of Mithra changes as much as the books imply.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}